Dependency order (the default, `--order dependency`) emits a module's rules
after the rules of every module it depends on, even when that reverses
alphabetical file order.

  $ cat > z_base.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".base{color:red;}"]
  > EOF

  $ cat > a_consumer.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".consumer{color:blue;}"]
  > let _ = Z_base.x
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate a_consumer.ml z_base.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .base{color:red;}
  .consumer{color:blue;}

`--log info` prints the resolved module order; `--log debug` additionally
prints the edge that caused the reorder.

  $ styled-ppx.generate --log debug --output out.css a_consumer.ml z_base.ml
  styled-ppx: output file: out.css
  styled-ppx: library order: .
  styled-ppx: order: .: Z_base, A_consumer
  styled-ppx: edge: A_consumer -> Z_base
  styled-ppx: environment: development
  styled-ppx: stylesheet:
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .base{color:red;}
  .consumer{color:blue;}
  

